WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  there had been a polio outbreak recently  in Borno in two years there hadn't been  a case  everyone thought polio was gone I was  actually following up on the polio  situation and started seeing reports  from the Borno government and others  that this huge crisis was underway and  everybody knew Boko Haram what's there  and committed terrible atrocities but no  one knew how many people had been  trapped living under Boko Haram control  the army came in and started liberating  areas that's when they realized that  there were millions of people who had  been deprived of everything for about  five or eight years almost 2 million  people have been displaced and they're  crowding into camps that can't  accommodate them and communities that  are so poor that they can't feed  themselves in the first place  a point five million people are in need  of humanitarian assistance 5.1 million  are malnourished  more than half of those children the UN  estimates that without treatment 75,000  of those children will die by June  probably Oh essentially is a sign that  the health system has collapsed malaria  you know child mortality was off the  charts and measles outbreak I was  horrified by the combination of  malnutrition and infectious disease  my name is Leslie Roberts and I'm a  deputy news editor at science magazine I  went to northern Nigeria to look at this  Medicare in crisis with support from the  Pulitzer Center on Crisis Reporting the  worst of the crisis is concentrated in  Boyle we visited Maiduguri the first  thing we did was go to the feeding  Center it was in a courtyard with lots  of women with their children waiting and  these were kids who had already been  screened and they had severe acute  malnutrition which is the worst kind and  if you're not treated one in five diets  but these kids were already on treatment  they're getting this they're ready to  eat therapeutic food then they would  measure the kids arm to see if they were  recovering malnutrition it's bad enough  in itself and then it suppresses the  immune system so when you get these  infections a severely malnourished child  was nine times more likely to die the  next day we took the UN helicopter to  monguno in the very northeast corner of  the state which has only been open for  about six months the areas around it are  still under Boko Haram  and the conditions here are even worse  than in Maiduguri  there we went to a UNICEF clinic and the  health worker would ask how long has it  been sick what are the symptoms the kid  would have fever and cough ordinarily  you use a rapid diagnostic test which is  a finger prick of blood but hers had  expired so she had no choice but to  treat everybody as if they had malaria  we went to that transit post this is  something that the Polio Eradication  Initiative has started fairly recently  they usually go door to door and  vaccinate kids but in Nigeria in this  crisis so many people are on the move  they find that they're missing a lot of  kids so they select in this case it's a  road that goes between two areas that  are completely inaccessible under Boko  Haram control and also heads into  Maiduguri the capital so they could get  people fleeing Boko Haram they could get  nomads anytime a woman goes by with a  child they fly her over and see if the  child has been vaccinated and if not  vaccinate the child  one young doctor took us to the measles  ward nobody knows exactly how many kids  are affected but he was saying I don't  need lab confirmation this this is huge  and he's seeing more than forty kids a  week first they have the rash the kids  have conjunctivitis their eyes are  swollen and if it's not treated and can  lead to blindness a lot have respiratory  infections and there were kids who had  to be on oxygen other kids have diarrhea  that's so bad their IVs some of the  children have sores in their mouth so  they can't eat which exacerbates  malnutrition in a developing country  without a crisis you'd see about 2  percent mortality and disasters like  this you see ten percent are sometimes  even 30  there are easy fixes for a lot of these  problems the biggest need is really the  screening for malnutrition and treating  these kids with food the army the Air  Force have to continue their fight and  then more money more a groups relief  groups you need to get in you need to  get access but the scale is so big and  so many are inaccessible still to talk  to the people about what had happened to  them it was devastating that they all  had tales of children they'd lost or  husbands had been murdered and it just  seems so appalling that nothing had been  done nobody has forgotten place and part  of it is there are so many crises all  over the world right now but they're all  vying for the same pot of money but for  some reason Nigeria seems to be at the  bottom of the list  [Music]

